1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:11,216
The Valencia beach show has stopped being a pile of follow-ups. Every job is visible now. Every deadline has a place. And when the plan moves, the board moves with it.

2
00:00:16,116 --> 00:00:29,343
Backlog. Ready. In progress. Review. Done. Priorities, dates, and checklist progress are visible without opening a thing. And when the work changes state, you move the card. That is the whole form.

3
00:00:34,116 --> 00:00:45,380
Open one card, and the detail is already there: what it means, how urgent it is, when it is due, what is checked off, what blocks it, and which records belong beside it.

4
00:00:53,116 --> 00:01:03,420
The same work can become a table, a calendar, a Gantt plan, or a dashboard. Nothing is copied between them. The board changes shape. The data stays put.

5
00:01:11,116 --> 00:01:21,953
Now, from the chat. We ask Francis for one thing: add a high-priority card called Print run of show, due Saturday, with three checks. We never open Projects.

6
00:01:26,116 --> 00:01:37,572
Francis finds the right board, creates the card, and builds the checklist. The tool calls stay visible, so we can see what changed. Then the new card comes back as a chip.

7
00:01:46,116 --> 00:01:57,871
And the chip is the door. Click it, and we land on the exact card: the right board, the right deadline, and all three checks waiting. The conversation has become work we can act on.

8
00:02:02,116 --> 00:02:15,343
The board does not live alone. The rain plan from Notes sits beside the work. The supplier sits there too. Next week, we follow those people into Contacts — the crew and companies behind the show.

9
00:02:20,416 --> 00:02:26,091
That's Projects — boards that move from chat, on your own hardware. Next week: Contacts.
